  pages={4206-4220},
  abstract={This article presents a noise-shaping successive approximation register (NS-SAR)-based direct-digitizing electrophysiological (ExG) sensing frontend (SFE) fabricated in a standard 180-nm CMOS process. To address the challenges of large motion artifacts and high electrode–tissue impedance (ETI), we propose three key innovations in our proposed SFE that enable accurate ExG signal acquisition with rail-to-rail input linear range (IR) and improved input impedance ( $Z_{\mathrm {IN}}$ ), while maintaining high energy efficiency. First, a 2nd-order passive-gain-assisted hybrid integrator with improved linearity relaxes quantizer resolution requirements, offering improved energy efficiency. Second, a prediction-assisted mismatch-shaping (MS) dynamic element matching (DEM) effectively alleviates feedback capacitor digital-to-analog converter (CDAC) mismatches and provides scalable MS order. The proposed prediction-assisted scheme ensures compatibility with SAR quantizers, marking the first SAR-compatible MS-DEM. Third, a highly energy-efficient dynamic  $Z_{\mathrm {IN}}$  boosting technique effectively enhances  $Z_{\mathrm {IN}}$  without static power consumption or additional biasing circuits, despite a large input capacitor of 12 pF. The proposed NS-SAR-based SFE achieves a  $Z_{\mathrm {IN}}$  of 81.7 M $\Omega $  at DC, a peak dynamic range (DR) of 101.1 dB, and an IR of  $1.8~V_{\mathrm {PP}}$ . With a power consumption of  $8.4~\mu $ W, it achieves a figure of merit ( $\text{FoM}_{\mathrm {DR}}$ ) of 182 dB, demonstrating its superior energy efficiency. Successful ExG acquisitions with motion artifacts in human experiments verify the functionality of the proposed SFE.},
