  abstract={The always-on drive loop with low power is a key enabler for intelligent inertial measurement unit (IMU). One of the key design challenges of the always-on drive loop is the trade-off between the always-on power consumption and the wake-up time. To meet this challenge, the burst-mode always-on drive loop composed of the burst-mode automatic gain control (AGC) loop and burst-mode phase-locked loop (PLL) was reported, which can theoretically achieve the sub- $\mu $ A always-on power. However, the parasitic relaxation oscillation (PRO) makes it vulnerable to temperature variation. To solve this problem the duty-cycle automatic optimization (DAO) technique is proposed, which maintains the gyroscope in semistable oscillation to enhance the thermal stability. The propose technique is demonstrated in an interface IC fabricated by  $0.18~\mu $ m BCD process and tested with a 3-axis MEMS gyroscope. The measurement result shows that this system has achieved  $0.8~\mu $ A always-on power consumption, 2.8 ms wake-up time, and ±5% wake-up time stability from  $- 40~^{\circ }$ C to  $85~^{\circ }$ C.},
