  abstract={A hybrid ring oscillator (RO)/ arbiter physical unclonable function (PUF) is implemented in a 28-nm CMOS, where two competing ROs accumulate a sufficiently large phase difference exceeding a predefined deadzone (DZ). The resulting eye-opening arbiter (EOA) architecture enables a prediction of PUF bit stability over temperature change (from −40 °C to  $125~^{\circ }$ C), supply voltage variation (±10%), and four-year accelerated aging based on a single point measurement at the initial PUFs usage. This once-a-lifetime initialization at nominal conditions generates a blanking mask without disclosing secret PUF information, off-chip or even outside the PUF cells. The EOA architecture additionally features an inherent multi-purpose auto-error detection (AE-Detection), which allows to assess the reliability of every generated PUF bit at every PUF bit readout. Using AE-detection and masking, a bit error rate (BER) of  $2 \times 10^{-8}$  over 16 measured devices and a total of  $200 \times 10^{6}$  measured bits over environmental changes and aging could be measured. The PUF operates with 0.259 pJ/bit, generates PUF bits with 4.515-Gbit/s bit-rate and only requires  $34.84~\mu \text {m}^{2}$  per bit, including all masking and stabilization circuitry, to ensure the low BER PUF performance.},
